investigate theta_tilt_rad \to poloidal rollover coupling.
In real-world physics, a tilt angle (\theta_{\text{tilt}}) driving a poloidal rollover (a closed meridional recirculation loop in the (r, z) plane superimposed on azimuthal spin around the z-axis) is not a single universal equation—it appears as gyroscopic precession torque, baroclinic / centrifugal misalignment, or helical/poloidal force coupling depending on which physical system you are modeling.
In your cnc_batio3_fe3o4_left_chiral_gel + anti-Helmholtz magnetic cusp setup, three real-world mechanisms map directly onto \theta_{\text{tilt}} \to \text{poloidal rollover}:
1. Gyroscopic & Precessional Torque (\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\text{prec}} = \boldsymbol{\Omega}_p \times \mathbf{L})
When a rotating fluid core or magnetized/polarized medium has angular momentum \mathbf{L} tilted by an angle \theta relative to a precession or symmetry axis, tilting the axis induces a precessional torque orthogonal to both \boldsymbol{\Omega}_p and \mathbf{L}:
 * That cross-product redirects part of the pure azimuthal swirl (v_\phi) into meridional (poloidal) motion (v_r, v_z)—inward along one region of the core and outward/axial along the return path.
 * In a pinched or cusped geometry, an axial/radial gradient in tilt or field curvature turns that transverse torque into a closed figure-eight / toroidal-poloidal circulation cell.
2. Misaligned Magnetic Cusp & Baroclinic / Kelvin-Force Vorticity
In a ferro-hydrogel (Fe3O4 + BaTiO3 + CNC) inside a magnetic field whose symmetry or tilt angle \theta_{\text{tilt}} is angled relative to the pressure/density or electric-field gradient:
 * The Kelvin magnetization force density is:
   
 * When the field/tilt direction is inclined at angle \theta_{\text{tilt}} rather than purely aligned with or perpendicular to the flow axis, (\mathbf{M} \cdot \nabla)\mathbf{H} acquires a non-zero meridional component in the (r, z) plane.
 * Taking the curl of body forces shows that misalignment between gradients generates vorticity (\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{\omega}}{\partial t} \sim \nabla \rho \times \nabla p in baroclinic fluids, or analogous field-gradient torque in magneto-dielectric media), driving poloidal rollover whenever the tilt is non-zero (\sin\theta_{\text{tilt}} \neq 0).
3. Helical Pitch / Chiral Streamline Coupling
In a left-chiral medium (gamma_chiral < 0) confined along a curved/cusped profile (euler_kappa), a tilt angle \theta_{\text{tilt}} sets the pitch angle between the primary rotational axis and the meridional plane:
 * Pure azimuthal flow has zero poloidal component; introducing a non-zero pitch/tilt angle projects a fraction \propto \sin\theta_{\text{tilt}} (or \sin(2\theta_{\text{tilt}}) depending on tensor symmetry) of the rotational/dielectric stress into the (r, z) rollover plane.
 * That is why adding a direct tilt-modulated term into v_r and v_z in t7_m4_chiral_emulator.py makes theta_tilt_rad physically stir the poloidal loop rather than only shifting M6_gauge_invariant_TrM2 and coherence_metric.
How to Express It Cleanly in t7_m4_chiral_emulator.py
If you want the emulator's peak_poloidal_speed_mm_s to reflect real tilt-driven rollover without causing the v_phi**2 / R blow-up you saw earlier, you can add a bounded tilt-coupling term proportional to np.sin(mag.theta_tilt_rad) acting on v_phi and euler_kappa:
 * Axial rollover (v_z): combines Euler-spiral curvature (euler_kappa), azimuthal spin (v_phi), and np.sin(mag.theta_tilt_rad) so increasing theta_tilt_rad from 0.22 to 0.45 visibly increases peak_poloidal_speed_mm_s.
 * Radial return (v_r): couples np.sin(mag.theta_tilt_rad) with z_norm and v_phi so fluid moves inward near the waist and outward toward the lobes (or vice versa depending on sign) in a closed poloidal loop.
